{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"0f847d157da858e226e4279ef6cb682b4b578260030a300870999afb8cd49f43","digest":{"component_or_area":"store event append","key_entities":["_store/event_append.py::_prepare_event","_store/staging.py","_store/lock.py::_acquire_mkdir","fsck.py::foreign_store_path_list","fsck.py::_scan","StagedEvent.promote","os.makedirs","os.rename",".tmp-newticket-*","batch_stage_and_commit","_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_translate.py::_write_event_file","_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py","_rollback_batch"],"problem_keywords":["atomic directory creation","interrupted write debris","filesystem safety","ticket directory","race condition"],"propositions":["Directory and first event file must be published atomically to prevent stranded empty ticket directories when the writer is interrupted between mkdir and the first event write","Staging directories use dot-prefixed naming convention (.tmp-newticket-<pid>-<uuid4>) to remain invisible to existing fsck scanners without requiring scanner changes","Both single-event and batch-event create paths use the shared _prepare_event function, so the fix at that point closes the vulnerability for both code paths","Event files are published via atomic os.rename under the existing write lock, ensuring events remain invisible until after concurrency guards pass","Stale staging directories owned by dead processes are cleaned on writer startup using process stamping (host + pid-namespace + start-time) to defeat pid recycling"]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787652928991440001,"uuid":"245acbd7-1a81-43aa